[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I verified the eBGP peering and data-path between the two AS border routers and found no ongoing instability. From pc1 to pc2, end-to-end ICMP works cleanly with 0% loss and ~1 ms RTT. On both routers, BGP is Established with one prefix exchanged each way: “show ip bgp summary” on router1 and router2 reports Up/Down ~00:01:4x, 1 prefix received, and “Connections established 1; dropped 0” (no flaps). Each router’s RIB shows the opposite LAN via BGP (router1 has B>* 200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2; router2 has B>* 195.11.14.0/24 via 193.10.11.1). FRR logs on both sides show only a brief TCP setup/reset sequence right after startup, followed by normal UPDATEs, End-of-RIB, and steady KEEPALIVEs at 60s intervals. The occasional “DENIED due to: as-path contains our own AS” entries are expected loop-prevention when a router sees its own prefix reflected back; they are not symptomatic of churn. Interfaces on the interconnect (eth0 on both routers) are UP with no errors/drops.

Affected component: None currently; BGP session on router1–router2 is stable. Mechanism: Steady keepalives and a single adjacency with no drops indicate no route flapping. Localization: eBGP peering 193.10.11.1 <-> 193.10.11.2 on router1/2. Root cause: No persistent fault; only a transient during initial bring-up. Corrective fix: No restart is required. If a manual session clear is still desired, you may safely clear the BGP neighbor or restart FRR on either router, but it is not necessary based on current evidence.
=== END ===